// Keystone-v1.0-RC24-Metal-Collision-Fix.dctl
// Keystone v1.0 RC24 production candidate.
// Exact DeSatch module behavior matched to Moaz Elgabry ME_Desatch.dctl
// commit 5e57387d486e82e416cf25bc8a95aad5e7f33c7a.
// That source is GPL-3.0. This exact-match integration is a modified/combined work
// and must be distributed under GPL-3.0-compatible terms if conveyed.
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
// RC7 keeps the HDR-safe primaries and native-linear gain white balance, removes
// the former Density/Chroma Richness system, and adds exact ME_Desatch-compatible
// global + six-hue selective desaturation at the encoded output stage.
// RC12 strengthens both White & Gamut Clean and Black & Gamut Clean: broader neutral eligibility, earlier tonal engagement, and a stronger perceptual slider response while preserving scene Y.
// RC13 adds Output / Skin Protect, using Keystone's existing Skin Target selector to shield skin from white/black neutralization while leaving technical negative-gamut repair active.
// RC14 adds a dedicated scene-stop Dynamic Range stage after contrast: Shadow Recovery, Highlight Recovery, and Specular Roll-Off.
// RC15 adds localized Black Point and White Point trims to the same scene-stop range stage and shortens the WB / Range UI labels.
// RC24 removes the manual Contrast Pivot: Contrast now derives its scene-stop pivot from each input transfer's encoded 18% gray, calibrated so LogC3 EI800 matches the familiar 0.435 grading reference.
// RC24 also extends White Point to -2.0 / +4.0 stops for stronger endpoint/contrast shaping.
// These controls preserve XYZ chromaticity, keep zero/default behavior neutral, and do not add Density or any hidden look.
// It intentionally contains no Technicolor crosstalk, palette transform, or hue rotation.
// Source-space roundtrip grader: native decode -> XYZ technical normalization ->
// scene-linear exposure / scene-stop primaries and tone / perceptual color -> native-gamut handling -> native encode.
// No universal AWG4 creative roundtrip. AWG4 is used only when AWG4 is the actual source/destination gamut.
// Creative defaults remain mathematically neutral. No display transform or hidden look is enabled.

